256
00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.720
And so yeah, we argued for like two weeks

257
00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:58.800
and in the end he was just like,

258
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.290
ah, it doesn't matter anyway, you'll be a stay-at-home wife.

259
00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.270
Again, he didn't mean it to be malicious,

260
00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.250
that was just his perspective.

261
00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:10.080
But when I think about how we show up and who we become,

262
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.050
it really does I think stem from who we were as kids

263
00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.490
and what we are told we are going to be.

264
00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.620
So even though I had these big dreams,

265
00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:21.600
my belief system overtook the dream that was inside me.

266
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.360
Yeah, so you come to LA to do this,

267
00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.074
it's New York Film Academy, right?

268
00:11:26.074 --> 00:11:27.065
Yeah.

269
00:11:27.065 --> 00:11:27.930
I know that they have that summer program

270
00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.120
'cause I looked into it for one of my kids

271
00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.770
where you get to be on the back lot.

272
00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:33.020
Yes.
At Warners, right?

273
00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.500
Universal studios.
Oh, at Universal.

274
00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:35.333
Yeah.
Yeah.

275
00:11:35.333 --> 00:11:37.350
And it looks like a really cool program.

276
00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.183
Yeah.

277
00:11:38.183 --> 00:11:41.430
And I didn't know that like, so Tom was like the teacher?

278
00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.890
Yeah, so what happened was I finished film school,

279
00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.690
so I ended up going to film school 'cause my dad was like,

280
00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.650
well, I'll study whatever you want,

281
00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:47.580
it doesn't really matter.

282
00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.950
So I go to film school and I didn't get the experience

283
00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:51.000
in movie directing.

284
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.670
Now I had the dream, I was gonna be the first female

285
00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.160
to win Academy Award as movie director.

286
00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.320
Katherine Bigelow beat me to it but anyway,

287
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.690
so that was what I wanted to do,

288
00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.810
so when I went to my dad after college and it cost money

289
00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:07.050
'cause it was a private, four week, eight week course,

290
00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:08.970
we argued and eventually he's like, all right,

291
00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:09.870
let's just make a deal.

292
00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.880
When you go there, when you come back,

293
00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.590
you're going to get a job and be serious.

294
00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:16.200
Now when he says get a job, it basically is a job

295
00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.450
to tie you over until you get married.

296
00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.040
Because then as soon as you get married,

297
00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.890
the expectation is you quit your job and you have kids.

298
00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:26.100
So when I had this course that I really wanted to go to,

299
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.840
he eventually said, yes.

300
00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:29.757
I go to America, I think it's gonna be an eight week course

301
00:12:29.757 --> 00:12:32.130
and I walk in and Tom is my teacher.

302
00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.230
Right. (laughs)

303
00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.260
I love how you describe him at that time.

304
00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:41.021
It just seems so different than the Tom we all know.

305
00:12:41.021 --> 00:12:42.960
Yeah, it really is.

306
00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.540
And that's part of my story and our story

307
00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:48.120
is that neither of us had growth mindsets when we met,

308
00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.710
we had very different lives.

309
00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.510
We came from very different backgrounds

310
00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.840
and that was partly the big hurdle we had to get over

311
00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.080
with my family where I was the first person in my family

312
00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.390
to marry outside of the Greek culture.

313
00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.330
And so all the pushback that we got from that

314
00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:07.290
and then having a growth mindset and developing

315
00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:08.850
to being the people we are today.

316
00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:13.110
Right, hence ensues this long struggle where you guys

317
00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:15.450
are just trying to make ends meet

318
00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.780
and living in a tiny apartment and driving a beater car

319
00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:21.300
and all the kinda stuff that happens when you're young

320
00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.370
in Hollywood and have a dream.

321
00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.580
But one thing that Tom always talks about

322
00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.269
and I don't mean to be continuing to bring it back to Tom-

323
00:13:29.269 --> 00:13:30.167
No, at all.

324
00:13:30.167 --> 00:13:31.867
It's just my frame of reference.

325
00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.770
It seems like he felt this obligation

326
00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:42.090
or had made this promise like I'm gonna create a large life

327
00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.200
for my wife.

328
00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:47.250
And that was part of how he wins over your family

329
00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:48.960
or your dad.

330
00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:52.650
And that seems to really drive him like his commitment

331
00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.960
to following through on that promise.

332
00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:56.580
Yes, that is very true.

333
00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.370
And that was kinda one of the early signs for me

334
00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.220
that he was very special in my life.

335
00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.350
So we wanted to get married.

336
00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:06.480
He went to my dad to ask for his blessing

337
00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.100
and my dad just said, no.

338
00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.080
My dad came from a very traditional Greek family

339
00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.300
so for him, he was like, well, first of all,

340
00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:14.610
the cultures are different, how can this ever work?

341
00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:15.990
What's gonna happen to your kids?

342
00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.010
You're not even Christian,

343
00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.420
you can't get married in a Greek church.

344
00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:19.800
And my dad was seeing all the things

345
00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:21.183
of why it wouldn't work.

346
00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:23.760
And then Tom, yeah, his famous was-

347
00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.060
Do you leave out the part about being an atheist?

348
00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:28.260
He did leave it out, I was like,

349
00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.960
babe, just don't tell my dad that.

350
00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.280
But he was one of those people he said,

351
00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:33.660
look, if this is important to you,

352
00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.250
I'll absolutely get christened.

353
00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.700
And it doesn't mean that you're changing my beliefs,

354
00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.710
so if you can just respect that I still believe

355
00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.060
in what I believe in.

356
00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.360
But if this is important to you and so it was those moments

357
00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.540
that showed who he was, the moments where he showed my,

358
00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:51.480
he just said to my dad, I respectfully hear you

359
00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.400
and thank you for giving me your opinion

360
00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.900
but I still plan to ask your daughter to marry me.

361
00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:00.840
Because my dad came from nowhere, like literally nothing

362
00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.030
and he'd built his wealth and success.

363
00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:06.120
A house roof over his head, that was what he saw in,

364
00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.100
so he turned to Tom and he's like,

365
00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.480
he don't even really have a job.

366
00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.430
Like at that point Tom was part-time,

367
00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.590
he was moving to England so that we could try

368
00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:16.080
and be together or temporarily moving to England.

369
00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.960
So my dad was like, you can't even support her,

370
00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.730
how are you gonna take care of my daughter?

371
00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:21.870
Very traditional.

372
00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:25.410
And so Tom famously said, look, I know who you see today

373
00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:28.140
may not be who you think I can become

374
00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:31.050
but I know what I'm made of and I'm gonna show you one day

375
00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.217
that I will take care of your daughter.

376
00:15:32.217 --> 00:15:34.920
And all of this is without me in the room

377
00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.170
but it was just the promise that he made to my dad.

378
00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:42.060
And it was I think, a beautiful push for Tom as well.

379
00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:43.050
He held onto that.

380
00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:46.920
He held onto the doubt and it wasn't doubt in the cruel way,

381
00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.160
it was just my dad just saw in essence, the facts,

382
00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.410
it's like, you've got a part-time job.

383
00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:53.603
You're not earning very much money.

384
00:15:53.603 --> 00:15:57.120
You are in lots of college debt.

385
00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.770
And my daughter has a roof over her head,

386
00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:01.530
she's very comfortable and you're trying to take her away

387
00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.440
across the world and I don't even know who you are

388
00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.570
and because he couldn't relate to his culture

389
00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:09.060
because Tom wasn't Christian and didn't have much

390
00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.280
of a cultural background, my dad was just like,

391
00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.720
this could never work.

392
00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.820
Beyond that, the bullseye that you're aiming for

393
00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.910
is like threading a needle that the success

394
00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.490
that you aspire to alludes most people.

395
00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.700
I mean, every year, busloads of people arrive in Hollywood,

396
00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:27.990
they've got a dream of being in the movies and before long,

397
00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:32.280
they're working some kinda part-time job or whatever

398
00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.300
and most of them flame out and leave or just settle

399
00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:40.500
into whatever career path is ancillary to the thing

400
00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:41.970
that they really aspire to do.

401
00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:45.030
And that really is the beginning of your path as well.

402
00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:48.660
Like Tom ends up in some job, you're home prepping meals

403
00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:50.877
and wondering what you're doing with your life

404
00:16:50.877 --> 00:16:52.590
and that goes on for a long time.

405
00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:54.930
Like I think people, if they Google you it's like,

406
00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:57.390
oh, Quest and like this huge success story

407
00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:02.390
but there was a lot of hardship during that period of time

408
00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:06.136
of just making ends meet, of course, but also this idea

409
00:17:06.136 --> 00:17:09.660
of you settling into this purgatory of the mundane

410
00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.130
which is a big theme of the book.

411
00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:14.430
Yeah, it was really hard and I think that's the thing

412
00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:17.790
I try to lean into now more and more and talking about

413
00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.130
because I never hit rock bottom.

414
00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:23.250
And how many people have you seen or met in your life

415
00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:25.410
that their successes came because they hit rock bottom?

416
00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.120
Yeah, I can raise my hand right here.

417
00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:27.953
Oh, right.

418
00:17:27.953 --> 00:17:29.910
So right, it's like you've got nothing to lose,

419
00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:31.680
so why not go for it?

420
00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:33.480
But what about someone like myself who

421
00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:34.710
I didn't hit rock bottom.

422
00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:37.530
If you'd asked me the time and people did, oh, how are you?

423
00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:39.870
Fine, I'm all right.

424
00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:42.810
It wasn't exciting, nothing bad enough to jump me

425
00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:45.270
into action and I stayed there for eight years

426
00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:47.160
and that's why I call it the purgatory of the mundane.

427
00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.250
It's like, you're this hamster just running on a wheel

428
00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:51.810
and actually not going anywhere.

429
00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:56.810
And so, because of that, I didn't think that I had the right

430
00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:01.803
to ask for more because I didn't hit rock bottom.

431
00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.220
And I'd had so many stories of other people

432
00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:07.110
who had real hardships.

433
00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.640
And so for the first couple of years

434
00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:11.460
where you had said earlier, President of Bilyeu Enterprises

435
00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.493
which basically was my title for being a stay-at-home wife.

436
00:18:13.493 --> 00:18:15.660
Right, it's like a whitewashed-

437
00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.280
It was, yeah.
A senior housewife.

438
00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:18.240
Exactly.

439
00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:22.050
So I used gratitude and I was like for the first year

440
00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:23.580
we just doing this for the greater good.

441
00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.110
Tom was gonna try and make enough money

442
00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:26.310
so we could make movies.

443
00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.440
So we kinda had a plan and so I was like,

444
00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.520
okay, I can do it for the grade of good.

445
00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:31.410
I don't like cooking, I don't like cleaning

446
00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:32.243
but you know what?

447
00:18:32.243 --> 00:18:33.240
I can do this for a year.

448
00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:35.280
I can do this for 18 months.

449
00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:38.040
And so, every time I would find myself unhappy,

450
00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.990
miserable, thinking to myself,

451
00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.960
how did I end up here when this wasn't the life I wanted?

452
00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:44.860
I used gratitude to remind myself of,

453
00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:47.520
I had an amazing husband, I had a roof over my head,

454
00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:49.140
so many people don't.

455
00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:54.090
Now year three, year four, year five, year six, year seven,

456
00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.690
year eight, that gratitude is exactly what kept me stuck.

457
00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.030
Yeah, it's a really interesting point that you make,

458
00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:05.030
the downside of gratitude or how relying on gratitude

459
00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:10.050
can prevent you from believing in something better

460
00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.160
for yourself.

461
00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.900
'Cause if you're always told to just be grateful

462
00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:15.960
for what you have then you're gonna settle

463
00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:20.960
or you're gonna be reluctant to reach for a higher place.

464
00:19:21.186 --> 00:19:23.460
Exactly and that was exactly what happened to me

465
00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.350
where I used it as an empowering tool,

466
00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:27.930
which I think it can be beautiful so that you don't

467
00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.730
just have a negative mindset all the time.

468
00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:32.250
So shift in that perspective can be beautiful.

469
00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:34.110
The problem was is that it stopped me

470
00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:35.760
from ever then speaking up.

471
00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:38.250
And so in year five and six where I was like,

472
00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.320
this is not the life I wanted.

473
00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:42.750
I was like, well, how ungrateful of you, Lisa?

474
00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.400
Your husband is out every day working,

475
00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:45.900
you don't have to work.

476
00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.150
I'm cooking, I'm cleaning but I don't have to go

477
00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:50.190
to a nine to five or anything like that.

478
00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:51.810
How ungrateful are you?

479
00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.030
How ungrateful are you to want more and say

480
00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.163
that you are unhappy when you have a roof over your head,

481
00:19:56.163 --> 00:19:57.627
when you have food to eat, other people don't.

482
00:19:57.627 --> 00:20:01.380
And so that gratitude piece absolutely made me

483
00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.233
stayed where I was and it didn't allow me to speak up

484
00:20:04.233 --> 00:20:06.090
and say, hang on a minute.

485
00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.440
Yes, I can be grateful for this.

486
00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.600
I can absolutely be grateful for having a husband

487
00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.760
that loves me and the roof over my head but that doesn't

488
00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:14.070
and shouldn't dismiss this other side of it

489
00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:15.690
where I'm profoundly unhappy.

490
00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.180
Like why do we dismiss other areas of our lives

491
00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.180
because we should be grateful for one area?

492
00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:23.610
Yeah, I think this is the most accessible

493
00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.661
and relatable aspect of your whole story.

494
00:20:26.661 --> 00:20:29.460
I mean, I think there's millions of people out there

495
00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.340
who are in some version of that reality

496
00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:36.330
where everything's kinda okay and we can just perpetuate

497
00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:38.490
the status quo, add in infant item

498
00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:40.980
and maybe next year there'll be a promotion

499
00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:43.530
or something like that and we can go on a vacation.

500
00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:44.940
There's nothing inherently wrong

501
00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:46.620
with anything that's happening,

502
00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.200
it's not really the life that I aspire to have

503
00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.780
but like who am I to aspire to that?

504
00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:54.060
Like, I have it pretty good.

505
00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.600
And without that bottom or that kinda intervening

506
00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.510
interruption like an illness or something to shake you

507
00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:05.510
out of your kinda daydream of the life that you're living,

508
00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.240
it's hard to have the level of self-reflection

509
00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.410
to make those changes.

510
00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.110
Like I know all the positive changes that

511
00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:15.630
I've made in my life have really been driven by pain

512
00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:19.080
and that pain can be emotional, physical, existential,

513
00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.450
but it has to reach a certain level of acuteness

514
00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:22.950
in order to get my attention.

515
00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:26.280
So it actually takes a stronger person and a lot more

516
00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.160
kind of wherewithal to have your wits about you

517
00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:32.760
to course correct when things aren't cataclysmic.

518
00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:33.720
Yeah, exactly.

519
00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:34.760
And that's why it's like,

520
00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:36.807
it really did feel like it was purgatory.

521
00:21:36.807 --> 00:21:39.360
It was like, I wasn't moving either way.

522
00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:41.160
So what is the inflection point?

523
00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:44.310
I mean, I know growth mindset enters stage left

524
00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:46.710
and Tom starts to develop this interest

